      "file": "scenes/scene-1.js",
      "narration": "Most slide palettes trip over the same things: too many colours, brand colours everywhere, weak contrast, and colour carrying meaning on its own.",
      "storyboard": "Ink-dark stage, a single mock slide in the centre. On 'too many colours' it floods with a rainbow of shapes; on 'brand colours everywhere' every element turns the same loud brand hue. On 'weak contrast' the text fades to grey-on-grey and nearly vanishes. On 'meaning on its own' two identical lines differ only by red and green, and a question mark appears. The four bullets are cut as text and become four quick visual failures, with a single label per beat in 90px type.",
      "minDuration": 10,
      "slide": {
        "title": "Why Most Slide Palettes Go Wrong",
        "text": "Too many colours. Brand colours used everywhere. Low contrast. Colour carrying meaning alone",
        "notes": "Most slide palettes trip over the same things. There are too many colours and brand colours everywhere. Contrast is weak, and colour carries meaning on its own.",
